@model Reflect.ProviderDashboard.Web.Models.ViewModels.Provider.Trust.TrustViewModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy and trust";
}
<header class="trust-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy &amp; trust</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">How client data is protected</h1>
    <p class="trust-lede">Reflect is designed around HIPAA’s privacy and security safeguards. This page is plain about which protections exist today and which are still the production target.</p>
</header>

<ul class="trust-status" aria-label="Current environment">
    <li><span>Data</span><strong>Fictional records only</strong></li>
    <li><span>Connection</span><strong>@(Model.HttpsActive ? "HTTPS active" : "HTTP: use the HTTPS address")</strong></li>
    <li><span>HSTS</span><strong>@(Model.HstsEnabled ? "On" : "Off in Development")</strong></li>
    <li><span>Times shown in</span><strong>@Model.TimeZoneLabel · @Model.TimeZoneIana</strong></li>
</ul>

<div class="callout" role="note">
    <strong>This demo is not a HIPAA-covered production system.</strong>
    A real deployment also needs signed agreements, workforce training, facility policies and a risk analysis. Those are organizational, not something software alone provides.
</div>

<section class="work-panel" aria-labelledby="safeguards-heading">
    <h2 id="safeguards-heading">Safeguards at a glance</h2>
    <p class="text-muted">Status reflects this prototype, not the final product.</p>
    <div class="safeguard-grid">
        <article class="safeguard">
            <span class="pill pill-ok">In place</span>
            <h3>Access control</h3>
            <p>Unique clinician sign-in, temporary lockout after repeated failures, one-hour sessions, and a caseload limited to each provider’s assigned patients. Accounts are invite-only: a facility administrator invites providers and assigns patients, and sees patient IDs but not names or sessions.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="safeguard">
            <span class="pill pill-ok">In place</span>
            <h3>Encryption in transit</h3>
            <p>HTTPS redirect, secure cookies, a strict content policy and no-store headers on private pages. HSTS is enabled in Production.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="safeguard">
            <span class="pill pill-ok">In place</span>
            <h3>Minimum necessary</h3>
            <p>Providers only see assigned patients. The assistant removes common identifiers (email, phone, SSN, dates and record numbers) before processing and does not save messages. Names are not reliably detected, so do not enter them. When an AI model is connected, each question is sent to that vendor; this demo uses synthetic data only, and a signed BAA is required before any real use.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="safeguard">
            <span class="pill pill-target">Production target</span>
            <h3>Encryption at rest</h3>
            <p>A managed, encrypted database with managed keys. The local demo store is not encrypted.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="safeguard">
            <span class="pill pill-target">Production target</span>
            <h3>Business associate agreements</h3>
            <p>Required with the cloud host, database, any AI vendor, and any email or push service before real patient data is used. Messages to patients are a prototype outbox; nothing is delivered, and future push notifications will carry no patient information.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="safeguard">
            <span class="pill pill-ok">In place</span>
            <h3>Audit trail and two-step sign-in</h3>
            <p>Sign-ins, invitations, assignments, record views, report exports and settings changes go to a durable, hash-chained log that records IDs and never patient names. Authenticator-app two-step sign-in is available to every provider and can be required for admins by configuration. Still to do: ship the log to external storage, enforce MFA by default, and single sign-on.</p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="work-panel" aria-labelledby="arch-heading">
    <h2 id="arch-heading">Where data travels</h2>
    <p class="text-muted">The production path. This prototype implements the dashboard, UTC storage and TLS.</p>
    <ol class="arch-flow">
        <li><strong>Reflect XR &amp; Mobile</strong><span>Session events leave the headset or app over TLS.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Ingest</strong><span>HTTPS API, encrypted nightly batch, or FHIR from the EHR.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Clinical API</strong><span>Authentication, audit and minimum-necessary access. The HIPAA boundary.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Database</strong><span>Times in UTC. Target: encrypted at rest, covered by a BAA.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Provider dashboard</strong><span>Shows times in @Model.TimeZoneLabel to clinicians, over HTTPS.</span></li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section class="work-panel" aria-labelledby="ingest-heading">
    <h2 id="ingest-heading">How data can be brought in</h2>
    <p class="text-muted">Three options for facilities. Only the first has any code today.</p>
    <div class="path-grid">
        <article class="path">
            <span class="pill pill-target">Prototype</span>
            <h3>HTTPS JSON API</h3>
            <p>A facility system posts sessions with a shared-secret header; times are converted to UTC. Currently @(Model.IntegrationEnabled ? "enabled in this environment" : "off until it is configured").</p>
            <details class="ingest-details">
                <summary>Show example request</summary>
                <pre class="ingest-sample" tabindex="0">POST @Model.IngestPath
X-Reflect-Integration-Key: (facility secret)
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "externalPatientId": "PT-10085",
  "startedAt": "2026-11-06T16:00:00-05:00",
  "durationMinutes": 24,
  "platform": "Mobile",
  "moduleName": "Breathing Mandala",
  "reflectionSummary": "Synthetic ingest example",
  "affectScore": 6,
  "hardwareId": "MOB-7740"
}</pre>
            </details>
        </article>
        <article class="path">
            <span class="pill pill-gap">Planned</span>
            <h3>Encrypted batch</h3>
            <p>CSV or Parquet files delivered to a private bucket or SFTP, normalized to UTC and loaded overnight. For apps that can’t call the API in real time.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="path">
            <span class="pill pill-gap">Planned</span>
            <h3>EHR (FHIR R4)</h3>
            <p>Encounter and Observation resources over mutually authenticated TLS, mapping each record number to the external patient ID.</p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="work-panel" aria-labelledby="tz-heading">
    <h2 id="tz-heading">Time zones</h2>
    <p>Every session and comparison window is stored in UTC and converted for display, so a local clock can never reorder history. Times here are shown in <strong>@Model.TimeZoneLabel</strong> (<code>@Model.TimeZoneIana</code>), and each record’s source details still show the original UTC instant.</p>
</section>

<section class="work-panel" aria-labelledby="cia-heading">
    <h2 id="cia-heading">Confidentiality, integrity, availability</h2>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table">
            <caption class="visually-hidden">How confidentiality, integrity and availability are met</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Goal</th><th scope="col">How it is met</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Confidentiality</th><td>TLS on the connection, role-based sign-in, assignment-scoped caseloads, HttpOnly cookies and no-store cache headers.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Integrity</th><td>Hashed passwords, anti-forgery tokens on forms, and UTC storage so order cannot shift with a local time zone.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Availability</th><td>Rate limits on sign-in and the API, health checks at <code>/health/live</code> and <code>/health/ready</code>, and HSTS in Production.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>
